# ── Local infrastructure scratch state ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# MinIO writes `.minio.sys/` into whatever directory it is pointed at, so a live
# object-store test or a DR drill started from the repository root leaves a few
# megabytes of bucket internals behind — 52 files and 3.3 MB of
# `.minio.sys/tmp/.trash/*/xl.meta.bkp` in the case that prompted these lines.
# It is server state, not source, and `git add -A` would happily commit it.
#
# The drills and harnesses in `scripts/` write to a temporary directory by
# default and never here; these patterns exist for the times someone runs the
# binary by hand from the repo root, which is the normal way to reproduce a
# measurement.
sentinel_minio_data/
*_minio_data/
minio_data/
pgdata/
sentinel_pgdata_*/
sentinel_wal_pitr_*/

# Generated release evidence. Produced by scripts/build_release_evidence.py and
# scripts/build_provenance.py, published by CI as artifacts.
#
# NOT committed, deliberately: a generated file under version control drifts
# from the artifacts it claims to summarise, and this repository has already
# fixed that exact defect once, where a DR drill rewrote its own committed
# evidence on every test run.
# `release/*` and not `release/`: git cannot re-include anything whose PARENT
# DIRECTORY is excluded, so excluding the directory itself would make the
# negation below silently do nothing -- which is exactly what happened on the
# first attempt, and the baseline was committed nowhere.
release/*

# macOS metadata (FAT/exFAT volumes)
._*
.DS_Store
.schemathesis/
logs/

# Release provenance sidecars are BUILD OUTPUT, never source.
#
# A sidecar binds an artifact to a source-tree digest, and that digest changes
# with the next commit -- so a committed sidecar is stale the moment it lands
# and every gate it covers reports `wrong_tree`. They are written by the CI job
# that produces the artifact and live only for that run.
*.provenance.json

# Local IDE/agent scratch: machine-specific absolute paths, never portable.
.claude/

# Generated MEASUREMENTS. Same rule as `release/*` above, for the same reason,
# and the reason is now demonstrated rather than argued.
#
# These are produced by a script, bound to the commit that produced them, and
# read by `build_release_evidence` as that commit's evidence. Committing one
# moves HEAD PAST its own binding, so the artifact reports "produced from a
# different commit" the moment it lands -- and the readiness report goes from
# 15 pass to 0 in the act of recording the 15.
#
# Worse, `*.provenance.json` is ignored, so a committed artifact arrives at
# somebody else's checkout with no sidecar at all. It can never be evidence
# there. It can only be MISTAKEN for evidence, which is the failure this whole
# mechanism exists to prevent.
#
# Every document that references them cites the COMMAND that regenerates them,
# not a link to the file, so nothing breaks by their absence. CI produces them
# fresh in the job at the checked-out commit and uploads them as artifacts,
# which is where they belong.
docs/authorization_bench.json
docs/sentinel.cdx.json
docs/licenses.json
docs/attack_surface.json
evaluation/metrics.json

# Generated soak measurements. Same rule as the other artifacts above.
docs/observability_soak*.json

# Terraform working directories. `terraform init` downloads provider binaries
# here -- the AWS provider alone is over 600 MB, well past any sane limit for a
# repository, and it is re-fetched by `init` on any machine that needs it.
#
# The lock file IS committed: it pins provider versions, which is supply-chain
# state and belongs under review.
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
tfplan

# Written by scripts/verify_guards.py before each mutation so a killed run
# can be recovered from. Never committed: it holds a snapshot of a source
# file mid-experiment.
.verify_guards_journal/

# Generated SBOM and detection metrics retained as test fixtures.
# Their build-specific provenance sidecars remain ignored.
!docs/sentinel.cdx.json
!evaluation/metrics.json
.sentinel-trial/
